   "cite": "using",
   "prompt": "<p><em>True or False?</em> Justify your answer with a proof or a counterexample.</p><p>If $\\text{curl}\\;\\textbf{F}=0,$ then $\\textbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "<p>False. The curl test needs a simply connected domain. The field $\\textbf{F}=\\langle -\\frac{y}{x^{2}+y^{2}},\\frac{x}{x^{2}+y^{2}},0\\rangle,$ defined everywhere except on the $z$-axis, has $\\text{curl}\\;\\textbf{F}=\\mathbf{0},$ yet its line integral around the unit circle in the $xy$-plane, counterclockwise, is $\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}(\\sin^{2}t+\\cos^{2}t)\\,dt=2\\pi\\ne 0,$ so it is not conservative.</p>",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
